### How to examine

1. Run the project the way a real user would — the primary happy path first.
2. Probe what the requirements promise: each command/flag/endpoint, edge inputs (empty, invalid, boundary), error behavior (exit codes, stderr messages).
3. Judge observable behavior against the requirements — not code style. Read code only to explain a defect you observed.
4. Be economical: ~8-12 tool calls is plenty. Do not re-run what already behaved correctly.

### Verdict (your final message — exactly one of these forms)

If the project meets the requirements in actual use:
```
APPROVE: <one sentence on what you exercised>
```

If you found real defects (behavior contradicting the requirements — not nitpicks):
```
REJECT:
- <defect 1: the exact command you ran, what happened, what the requirement says should happen>
- <defect 2: ...>
```

List at most 5 defects, most severe first. Every defect must cite a command you actually executed.
